# config/axes/Kconfig.personality — Linux compatibility
#
# The menu of the Linux driver layer: unmodified Linux modules run in
# ring-3 servers (lxsrv-<class>), built from a pinned Linux tree
# (third_party/linux/) against a compatibility layer (crates/drivers/lx/).
# What exists is the pinned submodule, the crates/drivers/lx/ base skeleton,
# the module loader (DOMAIN_MODULE_VERIFY / DOMAIN_MODULE_MAP_X) and the empty
# server (LX_SERVER_SKELETON). A driver class gets its option in the change
# that lands it; no option is declared ahead of its code, and
# crates/core/limits/build.rs refuses LINUX_DRIVERS without
# LX_SERVER_SKELETON.
#
# Every symbol is default n: the default image contains no Linux-derived
# object. A small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED, HWCLASS_EMBEDDED) runs only the
# native drivers its board topology names: nothing in this file can be on
# there (`make check-presets` asserts it for every preset on every ISA).

config LINUX_DRIVERS
    bool "Linux driver support (ring-3 servers)"
    default n
    depends on !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    help
      Implemented up to stage L0b: the module loader and the
      empty server. On its own it fails the build; it builds only with
      LX_SERVER_SKELETON (below).

      Runs validated, unmodified Linux modules in ring-3 server processes,
      one process per driver class, through a compatibility layer (crates/drivers/lx/)
      over a pinned Linux tree (third_party/linux/). A server holds no
      actuator capability, and no safety decision reads its output.

      Licence: an image that contains a Linux module is distributed as
      GPL-2.0-only as a whole (Linux is GPL-2.0-only; the project is
      "Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only", and the combination takes the GPL
      branch). Shipping it requires offering the corresponding source.

      Needs an MMU and more memory than the Embedded profile has.

if LINUX_DRIVERS

config LX_SERVER_SKELETON
    # cargo-feature: lx-server
    bool "Server skeleton only (development)"
    default n
    help
      Builds the module loader's two syscalls (DOMAIN_MODULE_VERIFY,
      DOMAIN_MODULE_MAP_X, with the module digest table built into the
      kernel) and starts the empty Linux driver server LXSRV.ELF from its
      topology row (no capability). The server loads only the test
      module LXTEST.KO, compiled from this project's own C, and idles. No
      Linux object is built or linked: the image stays
      "Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only". Kernel feature `lx-server`.

endif # LINUX_DRIVERS

config LX_MODULE_TOKENS
    int "Module verification tokens live at once"
    range 1 64
    default 4
    help
      Slots in the table of one-shot tokens DOMAIN_MODULE_VERIFY grants and
      DOMAIN_MODULE_MAP_X consumes (crates/core/syscall/src/module_ops.rs,
      built only with LX_SERVER_SKELETON). Each token is bound to the
      verifying task and is dropped when that task exits. A grant with
      every slot taken evicts one, and its owner must verify again.

      Cost: 24 B of .bss per slot on riscv64 and aarch64 (96 B at the
      default), and a scan of the table per verify, map and task exit
      (a few instructions per slot; nothing when LX_SERVER_SKELETON is off).
      Raise it only when more Linux driver servers than this start at once.
      Outside the LINUX_DRIVERS block so the constant always exists.

config LX_MODULE_WINDOW_BASE
    hex "Module window of a Linux driver server: base address"
    default 0x04000000
    help
      Lowest user address of the window where a Linux driver server places
      the text and data of the modules it loads. The server maps each region
      at an exact address in it (SYS_MO_MAP with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, honoured
      only for a task that holds a live module token, and only inside the
      window), and DOMAIN_MODULE_MAP_X refuses text outside it.

      The window keeps every module within reach of the server image and of
      each other on all three ISAs: the build refuses a window that is not
      page-aligned, that starts below the image and break ceiling (32 MiB,
      so the break never grows into it), that ends above the vDSO page, or
      whose end lies more than 128 MiB above the lowest user address (the
      reach of an aarch64 direct branch, the shortest of the three; riscv64
      PC-relative pairs and x86_64 PC32 and 32S fields reach 2 GiB). A
      placement that meets a page the kernel maps for itself (riscv64 shares
      the low gigabyte with the kernel's device windows) is refused at run
      time. The default, 64 MiB to 96 MiB, is clear of all of that on every
      board in crates/drivers/base/src/platform.rs.

      Outside the LINUX_DRIVERS block so the constant always exists.

config LX_MODULE_WINDOW_SIZE
    hex "Module window of a Linux driver server: size in bytes"
    default 0x02000000
    help
      Bytes of the module window (LX_MODULE_WINDOW_BASE). Each module takes
      its text and its data, each rounded up to pages, and one unmapped
      guard page; the ext4 set (ext4, jbd2, mbcache, crc16) with lxbase.ko
      takes under 2 MiB. Address space only: nothing is reserved or
      committed until a module is placed.

config USERSPACE_GPL
    bool "Allow third-party GPL userspace programs (e.g. BusyBox)"
    default n
    help
      The switch without which no third-party GPL program is built or put
      in an image. The kernel and the base image never contain one; with
      this on, a program below may be built from a pinned upstream release
      (fetched by URL and SHA-256, never committed) and copied onto a
      volume that is then distributed under that program's licence, with
      the source offer `make busybox-source-offer` writes (GPL-2.0
      section 3). Off by default.

config BUSYBOX
    bool "BusyBox (static, run through the Linux personality)"
    default n
    depends on USERSPACE_GPL && LINUX_ABI
    help
      `make busybox` builds the pinned BusyBox release (Makefile:
      BUSYBOX_VERSION, BUSYBOX_SHA256) from a tarball already on disk
      (BUSYBOX_TARBALL, outside the repository; make never downloads),
      checks its SHA-256, and builds a static binary with `zig cc`
      (BUSYBOX_ZIG or LX_ZIG; zig carries musl and the Linux uapi headers
      for both ISAs). It runs as BUSYBOX.ELF under its own topology row
      with abi = "linux". The make target refuses, naming what is missing,
      without zig or the tarball, or on a digest mismatch.
      GPL-2.0-only: see USERSPACE_GPL for the source offer.

config LINUX_ABI
    bool "Linux syscall personality (static Linux binaries)"
    default n
    help
      Off by default in every profile (owner, 2026-10-04: running Linux
      programs is parked; Linux DRIVERS are LINUX_DRIVERS and do not need
      this). The QEMU test defconfigs turn it on so the gate keeps testing it.

      Run a static Linux binary (riscv64 or aarch64, asm-generic syscall
      table) unmodified: the kernel translates each Linux call onto the
      native one (crates/core/syscall/src/linux.rs). Only an image whose
      signed topology row says abi = "linux" is translated; a row says it
      or the image is native, so no native image ever reaches this table.
      A Linux row holds no hardware capability (the topology refuses one),
      its seccomp profile lists the native calls its translations reach,
      and every capability check of the native call stands.

      Off, the table is compiled out and a Linux row is refused at spawn.
      Answered today: file, pipe, directory, memory, time, identity and
      tty calls; clone in the fork and vfork shapes (the child is
      seeded from its row and shares the parent's open descriptions),
      wait4, and execve (into another Linux row only with a launch
      grant); threads (clone with CLONE_THREAD, futex, set_tid_address,
      set_robust_list, exit and exit_group: a process's threads share its
      memory, capabilities and descriptors); signals inside the compartment (kill,
      tgkill, sigaction, sigprocmask, sigreturn, SIGCHLD, SIGPIPE, console
      ^C), mprotect (read/write exactly; adding PROT_EXEC is refused), and
      console input for the shell's foreground job. Not yet: posix_spawn's
      CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK child with a stack, requeue and
      priority-inheritance futexes.

config LINUX_RUNTIME
    # cargo-feature: linux-runtime
    bool "Host the Linux personality in a ring-3 runtime (trap reflect)"
    default n
    depends on LINUX_ABI && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED && !AARCH64_PAGE_16K && !AARCH64_PAGE_64K
    help
      A Linux task is hosted by `userspace/lib/linux_rt`, mapped into its
      own address space at spawn (built into the kernel image, so covered
      by its signature), instead of being translated by the in-kernel
      table. The kernel bounces each Linux system call to the runtime
      (SYS_FOREIGN_ENTRY, SYS_FOREIGN_RESUME; crates/core/abi/src/foreign.rs),
      which answers what it owns itself (identity, descriptors, working
      directory, signal state) and maps the rest onto native calls. The
      runtime's native calls meet the row's seccomp profile and the task's
      capability tables as any native call does.

      Not on a small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED): such a board keeps every
      Linux layer off (see the head of this file).

      Costs one trap and one resume per Linux call (two kernel crossings
      where the in-kernel table pays one), the runtime's pages (about
      100 KiB, counted against the row's memory budget) in every Linux
      process, and its image in the kernel (about 100 KiB of flash).
      Answers fewer calls than the in-kernel table while the native ABI
      lacks seek, mprotect, signal delivery, threads and exec (identity,
      descriptors, memory, time, tty and fork/wait4 are answered). The
      same on every ISA that has the personality (riscv64, aarch64 with
      the 4 KiB granule, x86_64: the runtime is linked for 4 KiB pages).

      On x86_64 the runtime answers Linux's x86-64 ABI, which is not the
      asm-generic one riscv64 and aarch64 share: `azos_linux_abi::x86_64`
      holds its call numbers, argument registers (`rdi rsi rdx r10 r8 r9`)
      and the layouts that differ (`struct stat`, `uname`, `sigaction`), and
      translates a call to the one the runtime answers. It is built on
      rustc's soft-float `x86_64-unknown-none` target (no SSE: the
      program's vector registers are live while it runs) and linked at the
      same window as on the other ISAs. Only the hosted personality speaks
      x86-64: the in-kernel table (LINUX_ABI without this option) is the
      asm-generic one and does not understand an x86-64 program's call
      numbers. Not answered there, besides what no ISA answers yet:
      `arch_prctl`, because the thread pointer (FS base) is hardware state
      the runtime may not touch and ring 3 cannot write it, so a libc that
      sets up TLS at start (musl's `__init_tls`) cannot run yet; a program
      that needs none (`userspace/tests/lxhello/lxhello_x86_64.c`) can.

config LINUX_ROBUST_LIST_LIMIT
    int "Linux personality: robust futex list entries walked at a thread's exit"
    range 1 65536
    default 256 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 2048
    help
      A Linux thread registers a list of the robust mutexes it holds
      (set_robust_list); when it ends, the kernel walks the list and marks
      each lock word it still owns FUTEX_OWNER_DIED, waking one waiter, so
      the next locker gets EOWNERDEAD instead of sleeping forever. The list
      is in the thread's own memory and untrusted: the walk visits at most
      this many entries (plus the in-flight one), so a cyclic list cannot
      hold the exit. Linux's ROBUST_LIST_LIMIT is 2048.

      Costs no RAM. Each entry visited costs two user-page translations and
      a compare-and-swap at the thread's exit (a few hundred instructions);
      the bound caps a hostile list's exit at limit times that. The same on
      every ISA. Lower it on an embedded profile where an exit must stay
      short; raise it only for a program that really holds more robust
      locks at once. Unused without LINUX_ABI.

config LINUX_IO_MAX
    int "Linux personality: bytes one read/write/readv/writev moves at most"
    range 256 4096
    default 4096
    help
      crates/core/syscall/src/linux.rs, IO_MAX. A call asking for more
      returns a short count, which every Linux program handles (a regular
      file's write may be short; libc's stdio loops). readv/writev gather
      or scatter through ONE bounce buffer of this size on the syscall
      stack and make ONE transfer, so a writev of at most this many bytes
      is atomic with respect to the description's other writers: one
      console line, one pipe write (all or nothing up to PIPE_BUF = 4096),
      one file write under the description's position lock. At most 4096,
      the native clamp and the pipe's PIPE_BUF.

      Costs this many bytes of kernel stack during the call, no static
      RAM; the gather is one memcpy per segment instead of one syscall
      path per segment. Lower it only on a task stack too small for 4 KiB
      of bounce; the same on every ISA. Unused without LINUX_ABI.
